From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheWeaklessOne <41053514+TheWeaklessOne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:26:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: vtdr6130-cog: per-unit refresh calibration for the
 Odin 2 Portal

The panel refreshes from its own oscillator, so a unit's real rate is off
from its mode by a fixed factor: one Odin 2 Portal runs at 120.55 Hz in
the nominal 120 Hz mode. A 119 fps stream then shows about 1.5 repeated
refreshes a second. AYN's Android corrects this per unit
(qcom,quec-refresh-120hz/60hz = 18/1974 in the panel node, a factory
value in /mnt/vendor/persist/odin/fps). ROCKNIX (PR 3484) and Armada
(PR 794) expose the two slot frame lengths as frame_len_120 and
frame_len_60 on the DSI device, calibrate them from vblank timing and
write the saved values at boot.

Do the same for the Portal with the same two attributes, so their tools
work unchanged:
- frame_len_120 (10..118, default 18) and frame_len_60 (1874..2074,
  default 1974). The modes keep their nominal timings for user space;
  each attribute's distance from Android's default is an offset in lines
  at that slot's nominal mode. A mode in the slot (above 60 Hz: the 120 Hz
  slot) gets the offset scaled by its vtotal, because the error is a
  factor on the line time: frame length = front porch +
  round(offset * vtotal / nominal vtotal). That covers the stretched
  gamescope modes and the switch while on.
- A new value is sent the way a refresh switch is (patches 0102/0103):
  a new optional drm_panel hook, resync_pending(), is asked on every
  commit by the DPU CRTC's atomic_begin; when it is true, the encoder
  waits for the previous frame's transfer and calls the panel's
  switch_mode() with the running mode. If no commit comes within 100 ms
  (the link is idle then) a delayed work sends it. A value written while
  the panel is off goes out with the next on-command. A send that fails
  stays pending and goes again with the next commit or after 100 ms, at
  most three more times, and the panel is put back on page 0 even after
  a failed write.
- The attributes were registered for every compatible but always sent
  the RP6's B1 table: on the Portal a write would have put the RP6's
  slot bytes into this panel. Each panel model now has its own ranges
  and writer; the RP6 keeps its immediate write, now under cmd_lock.
  On both, after a failed send, writing the value the attribute shows
  sends it again instead of being skipped as unchanged.

With both attributes at their defaults and nothing written, the commands
sent are the same as before: the frame length is max(front porch, 18) as
in 0101, the switch sequence is unchanged, and the new per-commit check
reads one flag.
